{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"Verified change. In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc (anthrax protective antigen) chain A was a single whole-chain domain, d1acca_ (SUNID 43828, description '1acc A:'), classified f.11.1.1 in class f (membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides): fold f.11, superfamily f.11.1 and family f.11.1.1, all named 'Anthrax protective antigen'. In SCOPe 2.08 the same chain is split into two domains. d1acca2 (SUNID 238522) covers A:14-225 and is b.179.1.1: class b (all beta), fold b.179 'Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like', superfamily b.179.1 'PA14-like', family 'PA14'. d1acca1 (SUNID 238521) covers A:226-735 and is f.60.1.1: class f, fold f.60 'Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like', superfamily f.60.1, family f.60.1.1. The whole-chain f.11 lineage was replaced by two new lineages, and the N-terminal domain moved to a different class. Both 2.08 domains carry the comment 'complexed with ca'. I retrieved no homology warnings, heterogeneity notes or repeat annotations for either of them. I did not query releases between 1.75 and 2.08, so I can't say which release introduced the split. The API did not give the reason for the change, so I did not verify the sequence, structural or literature basis for it. I did not retrieve any text beyond the domain boundaries and lineages.

What it reveals. A PDB chain is not necessarily one evolutionary module. A single polypeptide can contain several domains that are separate structural and evolutionary units, each with its own fold, superfamily and family, and possibly its own ancestry and partners. A whole-chain domain is a coarse, provisional unit. Splitting it into an N-terminal domain (A:14-225) and a C-terminal domain (A:226-735) is consistent with multi-domain architecture and with the 2.08 domains being reclassified separately. The two domains now sit in different classes (b and f) and different folds. SCOPe therefore makes no assertion of common ancestry between them, but that placement is not evidence that they evolved independently. The split also shows that SCOP domain boundaries and assignments are revisable. They depend on the release and reflect curation and the data available at the time.

Effect on homology searches. (1) Always record the SCOPe release, SID and boundaries with any result. d1acca_ in 1.75 and d1acca1/d1acca2 in 2.08 are not interchangeable, and old f.11 labels should not be mapped to the new lineages without checking. (2) Search and classify per domain, not per chain. A hit to residues 14-225 can support a PA14-like (b.179.1) relationship. A hit to residues 226-735 can support an f.60.1 relationship. A chain-level hit or a full-length match does not show that both regions are related to the target. Keep distinct aligned regions separate and check coverage of each domain. (3) A shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry of that domain only, and a shared family is a closer relationship. A shared fold or class means structural similarity only. Neither one establishes the exact function, ligand or mechanism. Calcium binding is not evidence that the two domains share an origin. (4) Use up-to-date domain definitions when building templates, benchmarks or training and test sets. Old single-domain definitions mix two lineages, so they can cause leakage or mislabeling. They would also make a 'family-level' benchmark look inconsistent. (5) A BLAST or FATCAT hit on 1acc chain A is comparison evidence only. It is not a SCOPe assignment for the hit target, and the 2.08 lineages do not transfer to a target automatically. In the absence of a hit, nothing can be concluded about the absence of a relationship.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a